    title: 'Producer Split Sheet Checklist: Credits and Ownership Before Release',
    metaTitle: 'Producer Split Sheet Checklist: Credits Before Release',
    meta: 'Use this producer split sheet checklist to agree credits, composition ownership, master participation, metadata, samples, and approvals before release.',
    excerpt: 'The split conversation is not paperwork after the song is finished. It is the release decision that prevents a good record from becoming an avoidable dispute.',
    tags: ['Articles', 'Guides', 'Artist Guide', 'Selling Beats', 'Beat Licensing'], audience: 'artists and producers finishing a song with more than one contributor',
    notFor: 'you are looking for a universal percentage or a substitute for a music lawyer. Splits are negotiated; this is a workflow checklist, not legal advice.',
    answer: 'A split sheet is a written record of who contributed to a song and what ownership, credit, and approval terms were agreed. Do it while everyone still remembers the session, before distributor metadata, registrations, and release plans turn a simple question into a dispute.',
    sections: [
      ['Separate the song from the recording', 'A beat, topline, lyric, vocal performance, and final master can create different rights conversations. Start by writing down which people contributed to the composition and which people have a claim to the sound recording. Do not let a casual phrase like “you have 50 percent” carry the entire agreement. Ask: 50 percent of what, paid by whom, and recorded where?', 'The practical rule is not to invent a standard split. Record the agreed composition shares, any master participation, producer fee or recoupment arrangement, and credit language as separate fields. If the parties cannot explain the difference, pause the release and get qualified advice before anyone registers the work.'],
      ['Capture the information before memories change', 'Get legal names, professional names, contact details, roles, publishing entities or PRO information if available, and the date of the agreement. Add the working song title, alternate titles, version name, and the file or session reference so the document still makes sense six months later.', 'This is not bureaucratic padding. A distributor, publisher, label, manager, or collaborator may see only a title and an email chain. A clean record makes it possible to match the people, the work, and the version without reconstructing the session from screenshots.'],
      ['Flag the things a split sheet does not solve', 'A split sheet does not clear a sample, transfer an exclusive beat license, assign a master, or replace a producer agreement. It can point to those documents, but it should not pretend that a single signed page settled rights it never addressed.', 'If the song uses a leased or subscription beat, attach or store the actual license certificate and terms beside the split record. A BeatPass certificate can help show that a beat was licensed; it does not decide how collaborators split the new song they made over it.'],
      ['Make approval part of the release checklist', 'Before upload, have every contributor confirm the final title, credits, split fields, version, and any publishing or master information being submitted. Save the signed file in a shared, durable folder and give each participant a copy.', 'The goal is not to make a small release feel corporate. The goal is to let everyone move forward knowing what was decided. That is the difference between a professional habit and a document requested only after money appears.']
    ],
    table: '| Before release | Confirm |\n|---|---|\n| Contributors | Legal name, role, contact, stage name |\n| Composition | Agreed shares and publishing information |\n| Master | Owner, participation, producer terms, approvals |\n| Beat and samples | License, certificate, source, unresolved clearance issue |\n| Metadata | Final title, version, credits, identifiers |\n| Records | Signed copy stored and shared |',
    mistakes: ['Treating producer points, publishing, and master ownership as the same number.', 'Waiting until distribution day to ask who wrote what.', 'Using a beat receipt as a substitute for the license and collaborator agreement.', 'Copying a percentage from another session because it feels like an industry rule.'],
    faqs: [['Do producers always get songwriting credit?', 'No. Credit and ownership depend on the contribution and the parties’ agreement. Do not assume a preset percentage; document the actual deal before release.'], ['Does a beat license replace a split sheet?', 'No. A beat license governs use of the instrumental. A split sheet records the collaborators’ agreement about the song created around it.'], ['When should everyone sign?', 'Before release metadata and registrations are submitted, while contributors can still correct a misunderstanding without pressure.'], ['What if a contributor will not agree?', 'Do not treat silence as approval. Pause the affected release or get qualified music-business or legal advice.']],
