function socialPackage(a) {
  return `\n\n---\n\n## Social cut package\n\n**20–40 second hook:** “The mistake is treating ${a.title.replace(/:.*$/, '').toLowerCase()} like a small detail. It changes what happens after you publish.”\n\n**Carousel:** Turn the decision table above into six slides: the false assumption, the checklist, the proof to keep, the common mistake, the practical next step, and the article link.\n\n**Community prompt:** “What is the one detail you wish you had clarified before a release, delivery, or collaboration?”\n`;
}
function operatingPractice(a) {
  return `## A 30-minute working session

The fastest way to use this guide is to give ${a.title.replace(/:.*$/, '').toLowerCase()} one focused session instead of treating it as something to remember later. Open the project folder, bring in the person who can answer the relevant question, and write down the answer while the work is still easy to identify. The goal is not a perfect system on day one. It is a record that another person can understand without rebuilding the project from memory.

Start with the artifact that will be used next: the audio export, release form, content edit, catalog upload, license record, or producer brief. Then work backward. What does that next step need to know? Who supplied the information? What proof or file belongs with it? Which answer is still an assumption rather than a confirmed fact? This keeps the review attached to a real workflow instead of turning it into theory.

### 1. Name the decision

Write one sentence that states the decision in practical terms. “This is the final version sent for distribution,” “this creator may use this cue in this show,” or “this catalog entry is ready for artists to discover” are all better than a loose folder of files. A named decision makes the next action clearer and shows the team what has not been decided yet.

### 2. Attach the evidence

Put the source material beside the decision: the license, certificate, split record, metadata sheet, approved export, briefing note, or platform terms. A link is useful, but a durable copy or clear record is better when access changes. Avoid making one person’s email account the only place the project can be understood. If the record is not easy to find, it will be easy to forget when the release is moving quickly.

### 3. Set the handoff

Decide who receives the information next and what they need in order to act. An artist may need a clean file and license proof. A producer may need the final brief and feedback. A manager may need an escalation note. A future editor may need the exact version name. The handoff is where good intentions either become a usable process or disappear.

### 4. Review the boundary

Finish by naming the one condition that would change the answer: a label deal, sponsor, sample, new client, exclusive request, changed version, or distribution setting. This does not make the work fragile. It makes the team alert to the moment when a simple workflow needs a fresh review instead of an automatic assumption.

## A practical before-and-after example

Imagine a contributor says, “Use the latest version.” That instruction sounds clear until a distributor, editor, or collaborator finds three files with the same title. A working record turns that into something useful: final file name, date, person who approved it, intended use, linked agreement, and the next person receiving it. The work has not become less creative. It has become easier to trust.

The same pattern applies when the answer is not a file. If the decision is a license, record the exact license and the planned use. If the decision is a custom brief, record the chosen direction and feedback boundary. If the decision is a catalog listing, record the metadata and delivery versions. The important thing is that the person acting next does not have to guess which conversation mattered.

## Keep the record useful after today

Good organization is not about making a project look official. It is about preserving the context that disappears fastest: why a version was chosen, what the parties meant, which use was approved, and where the proof lives. Give files clear names, keep one source-of-truth folder or workspace, and send the final record to people who may need it later.

Use a short follow-up review after the first release, delivery, upload, or campaign. Did the files make sense? Did anyone have to ask for missing information? Did a new use reveal a boundary that should be in the next template? Small improvements make the system more useful without turning every project into a policy document.

When the answer is uncertain, say so plainly. A practical “we need to verify this before we publish” protects more trust than a confident guess. That is the producer-magazine standard: clear enough to act on, honest enough to escalate, and organized enough to survive the next version of the project.
One final check is worth making: could a collaborator who was not in the original conversation take the record, understand the decision, and act without inventing missing facts? If the answer is no, add the missing title, file name, date, permission, owner, or next step now. That small test catches most of the friction that otherwise returns during distribution, client review, or a later revision.
The process also protects the relationship. When a producer, artist, manager, editor, or client can see the same facts, feedback becomes more specific and conflict is less likely to become personal. The record does not decide every creative question. It gives people a clean place to return when the project moves from an idea to a commitment.
That discipline matters even on a small independent release. Small teams change roles, creators move between platforms, and a track can be reused long after the original session. The few minutes spent making a decision legible are usually far less than the time required to untangle it after a takedown, a missed credit, a client request, or a collaborator’s reasonable question.

---`;
}
function render(a) {
  const body = [
    `# ${a.title}`,
    `Most people treat this as a small administrative choice. That is why it becomes a problem only after the session, upload, client request, or release date makes it expensive to fix.`,
    `The better question is not “can I move fast?” It is “what does the next person need to understand without guessing?” This guide gives you a practical way to make that answer clear.`,
    '---', '## Who this is for', `- ${a.audience}.`, `- Readers who want a repeatable workflow instead of a one-time workaround.`, `\nThis is **not** for you if ${a.notFor}`,
    '---', '## The short answer', a.answer, '---', '## The working framework', a.table, '---',
    ...a.sections.flatMap(([heading, p1, p2]) => [`## ${heading}`, p1, p2, '---']), operatingPractice(a),
    '## Common mistakes', ...a.mistakes.map(x => `**${x}**`), 'Each one is avoidable when the project record is built before the deadline creates pressure.', '---',
    '## What to do next', 'Use the table as a pre-release or pre-delivery check. If one answer is unclear, write it down, ask the right person, or pause the affected step. A calm, documented decision is usually faster than a rushed correction later.', '---',
    '## FAQs', ...a.faqs.flatMap(([q, ans]) => [`### ${q}`, ans]), '---', '## Next reads', ...a.reads.map(x => `- ${x}`), `- ${shared.start}`, `- ${shared.glossary}`, '---', `## ${a.cta}`, a.ctaText, `\n[${a.ctaLabel}](${a.ctaUrl})`, socialPackage(a), '---'
  ].join('\n\n');
  return body;
}

